[SYNTH_REPLY] The typical symptoms of GERD are heartburn and regurgitation of gastric contents into the oropharynx, and the disease is a gastrointestinal motility disorder stemming from reflux of stomach contents into the esophagus or oral cavity [18: "The typical symptoms of GERD are heartburn and regurgitation of gastric contents into the oropharynx"][18: "GERD is a gastrointestinal motility disorder that results from the reflux of stomach contents into the esagus or oral cavity resulting in symptoms or complications"]. GERD is recognized across multiple NCBI resources under the title Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease [1] and the MedGen concept Gastroesophageal reflux (GERD) [2], with the entity name Gastroesophageal Reflux [3]. Several publications address the condition, including PMID 29132520 [5], PMID 33351044 [8], PMID 36170502 [11], PMID 37034973 [14], and PMID 37516506 [17].

## Pathogenic Factors and Risk Factors

Pathogenic contributors include abnormal transient relaxations of the lower esophageal sphincter, defects in esophageal mucosal defense, impaired esophageal and gastric motility and clearance, and anatomical defects of the lower esophageal reflux barrier such as hiatal hernia [19: "other factors that play a role in the pathogenesis of GERD include defects in esophageal mucosal defense, impaired esophageal and gastric motility and clearance, as well as anatomical defects of the lower esophageal reflux barrier such as hiatal hernia"]. Long-term use of proton-pump inhibitors, the most effective pharmacologic treatment, is associated with bone fractures, chronic renal disease, acute renal disease, community-acquired pneumonia, and Clostridium difficile intestinal infection [18: "Long-term use of PPIs is associated with bone fractures, chronic renal disease, acute renal disease, community-acquired pneumonia, and Clostridium difficile intestinal infection"]. Potential complications of GERD itself include erosive esophagitis, esophageal strictures, and Barrett esophagus, a precursor to esophageal adenocarcinoma [18: "may cause erosive esophagitis, esophageal strictures, and Barrett esophagus, a precursor to esophageal adenocarcinoma"].

## Pediatric Considerations

In children, GER is common and often physiological, whereas GERD is a less common pathologic process causing troublesome symptoms that may require medical management [19: "GER disease (GERD) is a less common pathologic process causing troublesome symptoms, which may need medical management"]. Clinical manifestations in young children are varied and nonspecific, necessitating careful diagnostic evaluation [19: "The clinical manifestations of GERD in young children are varied and nonspecific prompting the necessity for careful diagnostic evaluation"]. A dedicated review titled Gastroesophageal reflux disease in children: What's new right now? addresses current understanding of pediatric pathogenesis, diagnostic evaluation, and management [12].

## Clinical Management and Ongoing Trials

GERD symptoms are among those most commonly reported to primary care providers, and clinical approaches include proton pump inhibitor trials as well as referral to gastroenterology specialists in specific clinical settings [20: "we discuss clinical approaches to the evaluation and management of these symptoms, including proton pump inhibitor trials as well as specific indications or clinical settings that warrant referral to Gastroenterology specialists"]. Clinical trials investigating GERD therapies include Famotidine in Subjects With Non-erosive Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease [4], Efficacy of Pantoprazole in Patients Older Than 18 Years Who Have Symptoms of Acid Reflux (Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease) (BY1023/M3-339) [7], Natural History of Gastroesophageal Reflux (GER) in Children and Adolescents [10], On Demand Treatment of Reflux Disease [13], and Treatment Of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease By Endoscopic Fundoplication, A Placebo-Controlled Study [16]. Additional PubMed records titled Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease are also indexed [6][9][15].
